 ## Background
-The field emerged from the need to animate 3D Gaussian Splatting representations, which were initially static. Early works like GaussianAvatar [S1] incorporated optimizable tensors with dynamic appearance networks. Human101 [S1][S2, unofficial] significantly accelerated training to 100 seconds. Subsequent methods like SplattingAvatar and GoMAvatar [S1][S2, unofficial] embedded Gaussians onto canonical meshes. Head-specific modeling advanced with MonoGaussianAvatar [S2, unofficial], GaussianHead [S1][S2, unofficial], and HeadGas [S2, unofficial], while hand modeling was addressed by 3DPSHR and MANUS [S2, unofficial].
+The field emerged from the need to animate 3D Gaussian Splatting representations. Early works like GaussianAvatar [S1] incorporated optimizable tensors with dynamic appearance networks. Human101 [S1][S2, unofficial] significantly accelerated training to 100 seconds. SplattingAvatar and GoMAvatar [S1][S2, unofficial] introduced mesh-embedded Gaussians. Head-focused methods like GaussianHead [S2, unofficial] and HeadGas [S2, unofficial] specialized in facial details and real-time expression control. Full-body and animal extensions like GART [S2, unofficial] and D3GA [S1] broadened the scope to general articulation.
 
